From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 24 Jul 2026 18:43:34 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 07854/11362] [outer-loop] submission gate: add SEED-STABLE
 CORE membership (additive, changes no verdict yet)

The 2026-06-20 CORE redesign states its intent in measure()'s comment: a knife-edge solve
flipping from order-perturbation noise must NOT count as a regression (A1/A2 lesson --
a 5%-recall no-op prune reshuffled a chaotic ~7800-action trajectory and dropped m0r0).
It delivered the COUNT half: a lever can no longer fail by solving a different same-size
set. It did NOT deliver the other half -- _verdict hard-FAILs on any (core - cur_solved),
and CORE was 'whatever the baseline solved in ONE run at ONE budget', so a chaotic
near-budget solve can enter CORE from one lucky run and then veto any lever that merely
reshuffles search order.

The pre-existing stability machinery does not cover this: select_headroom_budget /
stable_vs_1_5x check the BUDGET axis (does the solved SET hold at 1.5x) to pick a budget.
Raising the budget only helps a marginal solve, so it cannot detect instability along the
SEED / SEARCH-ORDER axis -- the axis a lever perturbs.

FIX TIGHTENS CORE, DOES NOT WEAKEN CONTAINMENT: stable_core_from_runs() = games solved in
EVERY baseline run; _baseline_core() prefers baseline['core_stable_games'] when present.
Weakening containment would re-open the A2 hole (trading core solves for fringe ones).

CHANGES NO VERDICT ON ITS OWN. Purely additive (45 insertions, 0 deletions); a baseline
without core_stable_games behaves identically -- verified directly against HEAD. Whether
m0r0 is seed-stable is an EMPIRICAL question needing a multi-seed baseline; excluding it
by fiat would be the 'relax the cap to admit a favoured result' anti-pattern and would
contradict the deliberate load-bearing assertion in test_arc_submission_gate_verdict.py
that dropping m0r0 must FAIL -- which still passes unchanged.

Tests: 7 new (incl. 3 must-still-fail controls: backward-compat m0r0 veto, dropping a
stable core game, trading core for fringe). New 7/7 pass; existing gate suite unchanged
(17 passed; the 1 teardown 'Memory leak' error is a pre-existing guard on a heavy
measure() test whose logic passes, unrelated to a pure function).

